Create and Understand a Git Repository

1. Create a New Project Folder

Create a folder for your project.

Example: mkdir MyProject

2. Initialize a Git Repository

Move into the project folder and initialize Git.

Commands:

cd MyProject git init

This creates a Git repository for the project.

3. Identify and Explain the .git Folder

After running git init, a hidden folder named .git is created.

Explanation: - It stores all Git information and project history. - It
contains commits, branches, configuration, and repository metadata. -
Deleting the .git folder removes Git tracking from the project.

4. Create a New File

Create a file named index.txt.

Example:

echo “Hello Git” > index.txt

5. Check File Status

Run:

git status

This command shows: - Current branch. - Modified files. - New
(untracked) files. - Files ready to be committed.

6. Explain the Three Git File States

Untracked

A new file that Git is not tracking yet.

Tracked (Staged)

A file added using:

git add index.txt

It is ready to be committed.

Committed

A file that has been permanently saved in the Git repository using:

git commit -m “Initial commit”

Conclusion

A new Git repository was created successfully using git init. The .git
folder stores the repository history and configuration. The git status
command helps track file changes, and Git manages files through three
states: Untracked, Staged, and Committed.
